                %li
                  %a{:href => "#in_bvbrst_formalism"} In BV-BRST formalism
          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#general General
          %p
            %em Perturbation theory
            is a general method of finding (or even defining) the solution of equations of mathematical physics by expanding them with respect to a small parameter in the vicinity of known, defined or well-understood solution (for which the small parameter is
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            One of the varieties of perturbation theory provides a method to make sense of and handle the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            involved in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            It is based on the observation that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of free
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            contains only the kinetic term, is well understood; therefore, the quantization of a functional consisting of a kinetic term and polynomial interaction terms may be expanded like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
            in the interaction terms, thus yielding what looks like a series of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlator"} correlator
            %strong coupling constant
            – the parameter in front of the interaction terms – is small enough, one says one is in the
            %em weakly coupled regime
            of the theory and expects this perturbation series to approximate the desired answer. Usually, even for that to work the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            first has to be subjected to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
          %h3#more_details More details
          %p
            Suppose we’re working with a quantum system that’s nearly a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+harmonic+oscillator"} quantum harmonic oscillator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_0 = \frac{1}{2}k x^2 - \frac{1}{2}
            is only a good local approximation to the real potential
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mn 0
                    %mo +
                    %mi λ
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_0 + \lambda V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mi λ
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = H_0 + \lambda V,
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is a function of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/position"} position
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            (or equivalently, of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mi i
                      %mi x
                    %msqrt
                      %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = \frac{p+i x}{\sqrt{2}}
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi d
                      %mi dz
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d}{dz}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            is small.
          %p Now we solve Schrödinger’s equation perturbatively. We know that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi itH
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi(t) = e^{-itH} \psi(0),
          %p and we assume that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi itH
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≈
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mi itH
                        %mn 0
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{-itH}\psi(t) \approx e^{-itH_0} \psi(t)
          %p so that it makes sense to solve it perturbatively. Define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi itH
                        %mn 0
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi itH
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_1(t) = e^{itH_0} e^{-itH}\psi(t)
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi itH
                        %mn 0
                  %mi λ
                  %mi V
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mi itH
                        %mn 0
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_1(t) = e^{itH_0} \lambda V e^{-itH_0}.
          %p After a little work, we find that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mi d
                    %mi dt
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d}{dt}\psi_1(t) = -i V_1(t) \psi_1(t),
          %p and integrating, we get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi i
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mn 0
                    %mi t
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi dt
                    %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_1(t) = -i\int_0^t V_1(t_0) \psi_1(t_0) dt_0 + \psi(0).
          %p We feed this equation back into itself recursively to get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ψ
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mi i
                          %msubsup
                            %mo ∫
                            %mn 0
                            %mi t
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mo [
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mi i
                            %msubsup
                              %mo ∫
                              %mn 0
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi t
                                  %mn 0
                            %msub
                              %mi V
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi t
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %msub
                              %mi ψ
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi t
                              %mn 1
