                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi nz
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \psi = z^{n+1} + nz^{n-1}.
            So at each interaction, the system either gains a photon or changes phase and loses a photon.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#DivergenceConvergence Divergence/Convergence
          %p
            Despite what one might naively expect, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+series"} perturbation series
            of natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            have a vanishing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+series"} asymptotic series
          %p
            Roughly this can be understood as follows: since the pertrubation is in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            about vanishing coupling, a non-zero
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
            would imply that the theory is finite also for
            %em negative
            coupling (where “things fly apart”), which will not happen in realistic theories.
          %p
            More in detail,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            configurations (such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            (such as
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            which as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"} coupling constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            schematically looks like
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
