            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "string%20theory%20FAQ#NonConvergenceOfPerturbationSeries"} string theory FAQ – Isn’t it fatal that the string perturbation series does not converge?
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general_2 General
          %p A solid mathematical formulation of perturbation theory has been given in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                K. Hepp.:
                %em Théorie de la Renormalisation
                Lect. Notes in Phys. Springer (1969)
            %li
              %p
                O. Steinmann,
                %em Perturbation expansion in axiomatic field theory
                Lect. Notes in Phys. 11, Springer (1971)
          %h3#ReferencesDivergenceConvergence On Divergence/Convergence and Non-perturbative effects
          %p
            The argument that the perturbation series of realistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
            necessarily diverges goes back to
          %ul
            %li#Dyson52
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freeman+Dyson"}> Freeman Dyson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Divergence of perturbation theory in quantum electrodynamics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/29799?ln=en"} spire
              %p
                %strong> Abstract
                \:
                %em An argument is presented which leads tentatively to the conclusion that all the power-series expansions currently in use in quantum electrodynamics are divergent after the renormalization of mass and charge. The divergence in no way restricts the accuracy of practical calculations that can be made with the theory, but raises important questions of principle concerning the nature of the physical concepts upon which the theory is built.
          %p and is made more precise in
          %ul
            %li#Lipatov77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lev+Lipatov"}> Lev Lipatov
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Divergence of the Perturbation Theory Series and the Quasiclassical Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://jetp.ac.ru/cgi-bin/dn/e_045_02_0216.pdf"} pdf
          %p recalled for instance in
          %ul
            %li#Suslov05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Suslov"}> Igor Suslov
                , section 1 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Divergent perturbation series
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/0510142"} arXiv:hep-ph/0510142
            %li
              %p
                Justin Bond, last section of
                %em Perturbative QFT is Asymptotic; is Divergent; is Problematic in Principle
                (
                %a{:href => "https://mcgreevy.physics.ucsd.edu/s13/final-papers/2013S-215C-Bond-Justin.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#BakulevShirkov10
              %p
                Alexander P. Bakulev,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Shirkov"} Dmitry Shirkov
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Inevitability and Importance of Non-Perturbative Elements in Quantum Field Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.2380"} arXiv:1102.2380
            %li#HollandsWald14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum fields in curved spacetime
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.2026"} arXiv:1401.2026
            %li
              %p
                Marco Serone, from 2:46 on in
                %em
                  A look at
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mn 2
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4_2
                  using perturbation theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J4nxvY1rOhI"}> recording
                )
          %p Exposition also in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Schwichtenberg"}> Jakob Schwichtenberg
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://jakobschwichtenberg.com/divergence-perturbation-series-qft"} divergence-perturbation-series-qft
          %p
            For the example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
            this non-convergence of the perturbation series is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Helling
              Robert Helling, p. 4 of
              %em Solving classical field equations
              (
              %a{:href => "http://homepages.physik.uni-muenchen.de/~helling/classical_fields.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            For the example of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
            this non-convergence of the perturbation series is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Helling
              Robert Helling, p. 4 of
              %em Solving classical field equations
              (
              %a{:href => "http://homepages.physik.uni-muenchen.de/~helling/classical_fields.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A general introduction on divergence of perturbation theory, asymptotic series and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            is for instance on the first pages of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcos+Marino"}> Marcos Marino
              ,
              %em Lectures on non-perturbative effects in large N gauge theories, matrix models and strings
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6272"}> arXiv:1206.6272
              )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Carl M. Bender, Carlo Heissenberg,
              %em Convergent and Divergent Series in Physics
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.05164"}> arXiv:1703.05164
              )
          %p Further discussion is for instance in
          %ul
            %li
              Physics.Stack exchange discussion,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/30105/is-qcd-free-from-all-divergences"} Is QCD free from all divergences?
          %h3#ReferencesInAQFT In AQFT
          %p
            Perturbation theory in the spirit of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
            is discussed in the following articles.
          %p
            The observation that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} Stückelberg-Bogoliubov-Epstein-Glaser
            local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrices
            yield a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+observables"} local net of observables
            was first made in
          %ul
            %li
              V. Il’in, D. Slavnov,
              %em Observable algebras in the S-matrix approach
              Theor. Math. Phys.
              %strong 36
              , 32 (1978) 578-585
          %p which was however mostly ignored and forgotten. It is taken up again in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              %em Microlocal Analysis and Interacting Quantum Field Theories: Renormalization on Physical Backgrounds
              Commun. Math. Phys. 208:623-661 (2000) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/9903028"}> arXiv:math-ph/9903028
              )
          %p (a quick survey is in section 8, details are in section 2).
          %p Further developments along these lines are then
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic quantum field theory, perturbation theory, and the loop expansion
              %p
                Commun. Math. Phys. 219:5-30 (2001) (
                %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/abs/hep-th/0001129"}> arXiv:hep-th/0001129
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory and deformation quantization
              %p
                Proceedings of the Conference on Mathematical Physics in Mathematics and Physics, Siena June 20-25 (2000) (
                %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/abs/hep-th/0101079"}> arXiv:hep-th/0101079
                )
          %p
            (relation to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Romeo+Brunetti"}> Romeo Brunetti
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"} Michael Dütsch
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %em Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.2038"} arXiv:0901.2038
          %p
            (relation to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+D%C3%BCtsch"}> Michael Dütsch
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
              = succeed "," do
                %em A local (perturbative) construction of observables in gauge theories: the example of qed
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://xxx.uni-augsburg.de/abs/hep-th/9807078"} arXiv:hep-th/9807078
          %p
            (relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
          %p Reviews includes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                %em Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1208.1428"}> arXiv:1208.1428
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                %em Perturbative Construction of Models of Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1503.07814"}> arXiv:1503.07814
                )
          %p and a textbook acount is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"}> Katarzyna Rejzner
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-3-319-25901-7"} pdf
          %p
            Further developments in perturbation theory in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"} AQFT on curved spacetimes
            icludes
          %ul
            %li#KhavkineMoretti16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Khavkine"}> Igor Khavkine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valter+Moretti"} Valter Moretti
              = succeed "," do
                %em Analytic Dependence is an Unnecessary Requirement in Renormalization of Locally Covariant QFT
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1411.1302"} arXiv:1411.1302
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://link.springer.com/article/10.1007%2Fs00220-016-2618-7"} publisher
          %h3#in_bvbrst_formalism In BV-BRST formalism
          %p
            Perturbative quantization in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-BRST+formalism"} BV-BRST formalism
            is nicely systematically discussed in section 5 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kevin+Costello"}> Kevin Costello
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Owen+Gwilliam"} Owen Gwilliam
              %em Factorization algebras in quantum field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.northwestern.edu/~costello/factorization.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            in the broad context of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebras"} factorization algebras
            (see there for further references). In particular the relation to Feynman diagrams is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Owen+Gwilliam"}> Owen Gwilliam
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theo+Johnson-Freyd"} Theo Johnson-Freyd
              %em How to derive Feynman diagrams for finite-dimensional integrals directly from the BV formalism
              (2011) (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1202.1554"}> arXiv:1202.1554
              )
        .revisedby
